<!--
  Adapted from the stock nav2_bt_navigator tree navigate_to_pose_w_replanning_and_recovery.xml -
  two changes from stock:

  1. Removed <Spin> from RecoveryActions, since a pure in-place rotation is physically impossible
     for this Ackermann car (steering has no effect at zero linear velocity), so it was a
     guaranteed no-op recovery attempt that just burned its timeout.

  2. RateController hz lowered from 1.0 to 0.2 (replan every 5s). This was tried once before and
     reverted because it interacted badly with an impatient progress_checker (default
     movement_time_allowance ~10s, required_linear_distance 0.5) - each "Failed to make progress"
     abort then had to wait up to 5s for the next scheduled path before retrying, turning quick
     retries into multi-minute stalls. nav2_params.yaml's progress_checker now has
     movement_time_allowance: 30.0 and required_linear_distance: 0.1 (see CHANGELOG.md's
     2026-07-14 (3) entry), which removes that interaction. Root cause confirmed via a live user
     log: with SmacPlannerHybrid producing a correct K-turn path (see (5) below) but still
     replanned every ~1s, "Passing new path to controller" fired roughly once per second
     continuously, and the robot covered under 0.1m of net displacement across a full 30s
     progress_checker window - each fresh 1Hz replan was interrupting the K-turn maneuver before
     it could ever complete, not just producing a worse path. Slowing the rate gives one
     maneuver attempt real uninterrupted time to finish.

  A third change was tried and reverted - documented so it isn't retried blindly: switching to
  "replan only if the path becomes invalid" (based on the stock
  navigate_w_recovery_and_replanning_only_if_path_becomes_invalid.xml), to stop continuous
  replanning at its source instead of just slowing it down. Real bug in this nav2 version:
  IsPathValid returns SUCCESS on the default-constructed/empty {path} blackboard entry before
  ComputePathToPose has ever run, so the Fallback skips ComputePathToPose entirely and FollowPath
  receives an empty path forever ("Resulting plan has 0 poses in it", "Controller patience
  exceeded", eventual navigation failure - confirmed via direct testing). Do not reattempt this
  without first fixing that empty-path fallthrough (e.g. an unconditional first
  ComputePathToPose before entering the conditional-replan loop) and testing it live.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="6" name="NavigateRecovery">
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <RateController hz="0.2">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
      <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
        <GoalUpdated/>
        <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
          <Sequence name="ClearingActions">
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </Sequence>
          <Wait wait_duration="5"/>
          <BackUp backup_dist="0.30" backup_speed="0.05"/>
        </RoundRobin>
      </ReactiveFallback>
    </RecoveryNode>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
